# Component Architecture & Variants
- Build flexible, reusable UI components using `class-variance-authority` (CVA) combined with `tailwind-merge` and `clsx` (via the `cn()` utility).
- Never hardcode arbitrary hex codes or pixel values in components. Always utilize the design tokens defined in `tailwind.config.ts` (e.g., `bg-primary`, `text-muted-foreground`, `font-sans`).
- Build components mobile-first. Use Tailwind's default breakpoint prefixes (`sm:`, `md:`, `lg:`, `xl:`) to scale UI progressively for larger screens.

# Icons & SVGs
- Use Lucide React for standard application icons to ensure consistent stroke widths and scaling.
- When importing custom SVGs, convert them to React components and expose standard SVG props (like `className`, `width`, `height`) so they can be styled via Tailwind text and fill utilities.
- Do not use `<img>` tags for vector icons.

# Animations & Transitions
- Default to CSS transitions and Tailwind's built-in animate utilities (`animate-in`, `fade-in`, `slide-in-from-bottom`) for simple hover states, mount animations, and micro-interactions.
- For complex, multi-step, or physics-based animations, use Framer Motion. Keep Framer Motion logic isolated to Client Components.
- Always respect user accessibility preferences. Implement `motion-reduce` Tailwind variants or Framer Motion's `useReducedMotion` hook to disable heavy animations for users who prefer reduced motion.

# Z-Index & Stacking Contexts
- Strictly avoid arbitrary z-index values (`z-[999]`). 
- Rely on native DOM stacking contexts where possible, or use a defined scale in the Tailwind config for layers (e.g., base, dropdown, sticky, modal, popover, toast).
- Use Radix UI Primitives (via shadcn/ui) `<Portal>` components to mount overlays, dialogs, and tooltips at the root of the DOM to avoid clipping issues.

# When implementing dark mode or themes, 
- rely on next-themes and CSS variables. 
- Do not use useEffect to check the theme on mount, as this causes hydration UI flashes.

